.TH RM 1.SH NAMErm \- remove files and directories.SH SYNOPSIS\fBrm\fR[\-dfirvR] path ....SH DESCRIPTION\fBrm\fRis GNU rm, from the fileutils.  It removes each file named.  A directoryis refused\fR(\fBis a directory\fR)\fRunless\fB\-r\fRis given.  A name that does not exist is an error with exit status 1,unless\fB\-f\fRis given..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-r, \-R\fRremove directories and everything in them.  Long form\fB+recursive\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-i\fRask before each removal.  Long form\fB+interactive\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-f\fRnever ask, and say nothing about names that are not there.  Long form\fB+force\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-v\fRname each file as it goes.  Long form\fB+verbose\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-d\fRremove an empty directory as though it were a file.  Long form\fB+directory\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfrm old.txt               one filerm -f maybe.txt          one file, if it is thererm -rv tmp/work          a whole tree, naming each entry.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fROS-9's own removers are\fBdel\fRand\fBdeldir\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOmv, mkdir